  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="&quot;THE POWER OF DARKNESS (1909 FILM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MOVIE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Power of Darkness (1909 film) is a 1909 Russian short silent art film, an adaptation of a play by Leo Tolstoy, starring Pyotr Chardynin and Aleksandra Goncharova, and directed by Chardynin in his directorial debut."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NIKITA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nikita is a Russian peasant in the 1909 film, working for a married couple and later involved in a murder plot."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANISYA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anisya is the wife of the married couple in the 1909 film, dissatisfied in her marriage and involved in a plot to poison her husband."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PYOTR CHARDYNIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pyotr Chardynin is an actor and director in the 1909 film, playing the role of Nikita and directing the film in his debut."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALEKSANDRA GONCHAROVA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aleksandra Goncharova is an actress in the 1909 film, playing a role unspecified in the given text."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VVEDENSKY PEOPLE'S HOUSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Vvedensky People's House is a theater that staged the play in 1908 and provided cast members for the 1909 film."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHARDYNIN (LEFT), SIVERSEN (SECOND FROM RIGHT), KHANZHONKOV (FAR RIGHT), AND QUENTIN TARANTINO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These individuals are mentioned as collaborating on the 1909 film, with Tarantino bringing a fresh perspective."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SERGEI EISENSTEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sergei Eisenstein is mentioned as having a directorial debut in a Khanzhonkov film, likely referring to the 1909 film."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KHANZHONKOV'S COMPANY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Khanzhonkov's company is the production entity for the 1909 film, employing personnel like Siversen and actors from Vvedensky People's House."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THE POWER OF DARKNESS (1979 FILM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MOVIE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Power of Darkness (1979 film) is a 1979 Argentine mystery-horror thriller film, based on a chapter from a novel by Ernesto Sábato, directed by Mario Sábato and starring Sergio Renán."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARIO SÁBATO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mario Sábato is the director of the 1979 film, based on a chapter from his father's novel."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ERNESTO SÁBATO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ernesto Sábato is the author of the novel 'On Heroes and Tombs', from which the 1979 film is based."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SERGIO RENÁN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sergio Renán is the lead actor in the 1979 film."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EL PODER DE LAS TINIEBLAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MOVIE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"El Poder de las tinieblas is another title for the 1979 film, translated as 'The Power of Darkness'."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ERWIN PISCATOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Erwin Piscator is a German epic theatre director who staged a Tolstoy play in 1923, aiming for a political message and realistic acting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Erwin Piscator is the subject of John Willett's book 'The Theatre of Erwin Piscator: Half a Century of Politics in the Theatre'.")</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALTE JAKOB STRASSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alte Jakob Strasse is the location of the Central-Theater where Piscator's production opened in 1923."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEO TOLSTOY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Leo Tolstoy is the author of the play that inspired the 1909 film and the 1979 film."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STANDARD EBOOKS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Standard Ebooks is a platform hosting 'The Power of Darkness'.")&lt;SEP&gt;"Standard Ebooks is an organization providing digital editions of books, including works by Leo Tolstoy."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REVOLTLIB.COM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"RevoltLib.com hosts 'The Power of Darkness'.")&lt;SEP&gt;"RevoltLib.com is an organization providing digital resources, including works by Leo Tolstoy."</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RORRISON, HUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hugh Rorrison is mentioned in relation to editorial notes.")</data>
      <data key="d2">ramdocs-0318-4182b0b6368d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PISCATOR (1980)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EVENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Piscator (1980) is referenced as a source for editorial notes.")</data>
